# [[if .Org]][[.Org]] [[end]]Claude Code profiles

This is the **org data repo** of [[if .Org]][[.Org]]: [[else]]this organization: [[end]]the profiles developers start Claude Code with, and the CI that checks them. It has no plugin marketplace and no plugin catalog (`[catalog] enabled = false` in `ccshelf.toml`). You manage it with [ccshelf](https://github.com/yorch/ccshelf). `ccshelf catalog init --profiles-only` generated it.

## Layout

```
profiles/<name>.toml               profile manifests (a set of plugins, skills and MCP servers per role)
mcp/registry.toml                  MCP servers that profiles may name (optional)
prompts/<name>.md                  system prompt files that profiles may use (optional)
ccshelf.toml                       org configuration: lint rules, protected plugins and MCP servers
.github/CODEOWNERS                 who reviews what
.github/workflows/validate.yml     runs ccshelf lint on pull requests
```

## Set up (once)

- [ ] **Pin ccshelf in the workflow.** `validate.yml` calls the `yorch/ccshelf/action`. In it:
  1. Replace the all-zero commit SHA with the full 40-character commit SHA of the ccshelf release you chose, and keep its tag in the trailing comment.
  2. Set `version:` to the same release.
  3. Delete the `guard` job and the `needs: guard` line that sit above and inside the first job.

  While the placeholder is there, the `guard` job fails with a clear `ccshelf is not pinned` error and the other job is skipped. Without the `guard` job, the runner would stop with "unable to resolve action", because it resolves every action of a job before it runs the first step. Prefer a commit that already holds the `action/pins.txt` line for your version: then the commit alone pins the binary. Optionally, set the repository variable `CCSHELF_SHA256_LINUX_AMD64` to the archive hash from the release's `checksums.txt`.
- [ ] **Protect the repository.** Require code-owner review on the default branch ([[.BranchCode]]). The platform team owns `.github/`, so nobody else can change the workflow or CODEOWNERS.
- [ ] **Decide who the platform owners are.** [[.PlatformOwners]] own everything that decides what runs on developer machines: profiles, the MCP registry, prompts, the workflow and this configuration.
- [ ] **Choose which plugins and MCP servers nobody may mask.** List them under `[protect]` in `ccshelf.toml`.

## Add a profile

1. Create `profiles/<name>.toml` (`name` must equal the file name without `.toml`). `profiles/example.toml.sample`, when it was generated, shows the format. ccshelf has no built-in profiles: the roles are your choice.
2. Run `ccshelf lint` to check it.
3. Open a pull request. CI runs `ccshelf lint`.
4. Developers add this repository as a profile source (`ccshelf init`, or `ccshelf config source add`) and start Claude Code with `ccshelf run <name>`.

## Everyday commands

```
ccshelf lint                          check the profiles, the MCP registry and ownership
```

`ccshelf compile` and `ccshelf catalog build` need a plugin marketplace and refuse to run here. To publish a plugin catalog later, remove `enabled = false` from `[catalog]` and run `ccshelf catalog init` again without `--profiles-only`.

## GitHub Enterprise Server

The workflow uses no host name. On GitHub Enterprise Server the administrator must allow or mirror the actions it uses.

Documentation: <https://github.com/yorch/ccshelf>.
